            "timestamp": 1559570900,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Alexander Amelkin",
                "username": "AlexanderAmelkin"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 1:\n\nThis is a counterpart of https://gerrit.openbmc-project.xyz/c/openbmc/phosphor-net-ipmid/+/22205\n\nPlease also see this discussion (on existence of counterparts):\nhttps://lists.ozlabs.org/pipermail/openbmc/2019-May/016425.html"
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1559697335,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Emily Shaffer",
                "email": "emilyshaffer@google.com",
                "username": "nasamuffin"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 1:\n\n(7 comments)\n\nAlexander, I just scanned this briefly, so let me summarize what I think is going on - and please correct me if/when I am wrong.\n\nI think this is talking about the System Boot Options commands.\nIt looks like some interfaces have duplicate objects on the DBus, e.g. \"xyz/openbmc/foo/Bar\" and \"xyz/openbmc/foo/oneTime/Bar\".\nBased on that pattern, we're assuming that if there are two Bar objects found, one of them is a one-time setting (I imagine this one-time setting is for asking for specific options on next boot, such as choosing boot device).\nMaybe the other is a persistent setting, for example, if you usually want to boot from flash but just this once you want to boot from network? \nThe change you've made accounts for some objects which can only be set persistently, like Boot Initiator Mailbox, which previously would not have worked as there is no one-time setting object on DBus.\n\nI suppose my concerns about this change all lie in the assumptions and design decisions which were made before you got your hands on it. Your change seems mostly good with the existing implementation.\n\nHowever, I don't like the existing implementation. It seems to be making a brittle assumption about the way settings will be advertised via DBus. I'd prefer if we were to grep the settings objects more aggressively - I mean, what if we discover some setting we want to expose only as a one-time setting? Then, while I guess your new logic will work correctly, the semantics described don't really make a difference.\n\nI'll make some comments inline that I think might help..."
        },
